WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.169 --> 00:00:27.009
Your screen is currently filling up with clouds
of an algae that resembles a unicellular flagellate

00:00:27.009 --> 00:00:32.559
known as Chlamydomonas, though we’re not
quite sure that that’s definitely what it

00:00:32.559 --> 00:00:33.559
is.

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:38.579
The algae are swimming towards the lit part
of the stage, and as they amass, they form

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:49.270
a blanket over the slide that looks like a
kind of very festive green television static.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:57.379
Meanwhile, on the more orderly end of things,
we have this Volvox.

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:03.729
It’s much larger than the tiny algae we
just saw, and it’s also much more serene.

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:07.130
Just a nice chill green ball in the middle
of your screen.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.320
It doesn’t seem to have much in common with
the Chalmydomonas.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.590
I mean, yes, they’ve got some green in common.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:18.070
But that’s not particularly difficult to
come by in the microcosmos.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:25.720
But these two organisms are both part of the
same family of algae called volvocine algae.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.500
And when you look at a more extended family
portrait, maybe you will start to see the

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.500
resemblance.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.020
You’ve got your gonium, looking like several
of those little green algae stuck together.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.820
And then there’s this active pandorina,
also packing together several green cells

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.840
in its colony body as it moves around.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.549
And then we see the Eudorina….and then the
pleodorina, which looks increasingly like

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:58.039
the orderly sphere of the Volvox
In fact, this combination of footage feels

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:04.859
less like a family portrait and more like
a series of yearbook photos taken from kindergarten

00:02:04.859 --> 00:02:06.819
through senior year.

00:02:06.819 --> 00:02:12.680
Except instead of the striking passage of
time documented through the changes in a child’s

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:19.530
face, this is the striking passage of multicellular
evolution documented through the changes in

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.030
one algal family.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:28.430
Multicellularity is one of those things that
is so entwined with notions of complexity

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:34.540
and the forward marching of evolution that
it might feel like it has to be one of those

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:41.319
singular evolutionary events: a moment that
occurred once or maybe twice, and was maybe

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:44.400
too difficult to ever replicate again.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:50.130
But it turns out that making the jump from
unicellular to multicellular life has actually

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:54.390
happened quite a few times in our planet’s
history.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:58.970
It’s maybe surprisingly not that difficult
a transition.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:05.349
But finding a way to study it is.

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:11.760
Many of these jumps happened too long ago,
and evolution has removed the organisms whose

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:18.360
bodies contain the story of how they or their
ancestors went from living on their own to

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:19.360
teaming up with others.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.720
But fortunately, the volvocine algae went
about this transition much more recently--likely

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.160
around 200 million years ago.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:33.960
And that leaves us with a gradient of cellular
complexity that starts with the unicellular

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:39.349
Chlamydomonas, transitions to the colonial
gatherings of cells that make up the gonium

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:44.860
through pleodorina, and then leads us to the
multicellular Volvox.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:49.189
That ordering though is a little misleading
in its simplicity.

00:03:49.189 --> 00:03:54.040
Evolution isn’t nearly so neat to work in
a straight line across a whole family.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:04:01.360
But laying out the organisms in this way helps
us to understand that steps are involved in

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.939
taking the species from unicellular to multicellular.

00:04:05.939 --> 00:04:11.170
One of the most apparent differences when
we compare those single-celled algae to Volvox

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.650
is how much bigger the Volvox is.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:19.380
The world is made up of large unicellular
organisms and small multicellular organisms.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:25.090
But in this family, there is an increase in
size that is connected to how many cells an

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.330
individual contains.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.260
So, how to do that?

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:34.780
Well, volvocine algae primarily reproduce
asexually.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:39.680
But where many microbes divide through binary
fission--dividing into two daughter cells

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.490
that then grow and divide again--most volvocine
algae are a little more chaotic.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:51.029
They divide through multiple fission, which
means that any cell that’s going to reproduce

00:04:51.029 --> 00:04:54.780
starts by growing...a lot.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.680
If they want two daughter cells, they grow
twice as big.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.330
If they want to make four, they grow four
times as large.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:06.940
And once they get as big as they need to,
the algae goes through a rapid series of divisions

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.450
to produce all those daughter cells in quick
succession.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:14.220
For Chlamydomonas, you might get around 4
- 8 daughter cells.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.910
A gonium colony might have 8 - 16.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:21.820
And with Volvox, you can end up with thousands.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:26.850
And more than just the sheer increase in numbers
is what the daughter cells do when they are

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.360
newly made.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:34.539
In the Chlamydomonas algae, they separate
completely through a process called cytokinesis,

00:05:34.539 --> 00:05:36.960
becoming their own individual self.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:43.140
But with the colonial algae and on up, that
cytokinesis is incomplete, leaving the cells

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.930
connected.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:52.590
Those connections are further supported by
an extracellular matrix made up of glycoproteins.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:58.729
And as the number of cells increased through
evolution, so too did the volume of that matrix.

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:05.170
At certain points in a Volvox’s life cycle,
that transparent scaffolding can make up 99%

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:07.940
of its volume.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:11.740
But multicellular is more than just getting
big.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:18.399
The big step to taking the volvocine algae
from colony to multicellular life involves

00:06:18.399 --> 00:06:23.550
putting all those cells to work--specialized
work.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:28.480
You can see the early days of specialization
in the colonial algae through the development

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:34.830
of organismal polarity, which is a fancy way
of saying that we can define a front and back

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.490
of the colony.

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:41.060
The cells in front are generally large with
big eyespots.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:45.160
And as you go to the back, the cells and eyespots
get smaller.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:50.839
These differences create a gradient not just
in characteristics, but in function.

00:06:50.839 --> 00:06:56.880
The larger eyespots in front mean those cells
will be more sensitive to light, making them

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:59.690
responsible for directing the colony.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:05.250
With the Volvox, this specialization transforms
into a full division labor that you can actually

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.820
see reflected in the cells themselves.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:14.680
Its globe-like shape features thousands of
tiny cells dotting the exterior, all orbiting

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:17.659
a few large cells held in the middle.

00:07:17.659 --> 00:07:25.240
Those are two distinct cell types: the thousands
of cells on the outside are somatic cells,

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.790
decorated with flagella to help the organism
move around.

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:34.039
And there may be thousands of these somatic
cells, but they can’t do the most important

00:07:34.039 --> 00:07:38.330
thing an organism needs to do, which is reproduce.

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:43.130
Instead, that role is given to those large
cells called gonidia.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:49.190
At some point in the Volvox’s life, those
gonidia will each divide to produce a new

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:53.380
Volvox, which is essential to keeping the
Volvox lineage going.

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:57.870
The one thing the gonidia can’t do is swim.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.250
They don’t have the flagella for it.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:03.070
And that’s why they need somatic cells.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:09.530
It is a full division of labor, one that you’ll
find in many forms of multicellular organisms

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:11.350
all over the planet.

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:12.729
But why?

00:08:12.729 --> 00:08:14.710
Why go through all of this?

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:16.990
Well, there’s a lot of possible answers.

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:22.740
But one of the simplest answers is probably
captured by this tardigrade idly grazing on

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:28.540
algae, looking a bit more like a “water
cow” than a “water bear” here.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:32.330
This Chlamydomonas algae does not stand a
chance.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:34.839
But you know who does stand a chance?

00:08:34.839 --> 00:08:35.839
A pandorina.

00:08:35.839 --> 00:08:40.289
It’s simply too large to get into the tardigrade’s
mouth.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:46.649
And in this moment, hundreds of millions of
years of evolution--of cells dividing, of

00:08:46.649 --> 00:08:52.860
being held together through incomplete cytokinesis,
of biologically defining front and back--all

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:59.759
of that comes together to save this little
guy’s life.

00:08:59.759 --> 00:09:05.470
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:06.470
us.

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:11.060
We have some very exciting news to share with
you!

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:13.970
You know that unseen world I just mentioned?

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:19.930
Well we want to help you see that world and
share it with us and with others!

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:25.480
We’ve worked together with our Master of
Microscopes James Weiss to design a microscope

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:31.889
with content creation in mind, and you can
now help us make that microscope through a

00:09:31.889 --> 00:09:33.459
Kickstarter.

00:09:33.459 --> 00:09:39.050
We want people to be able to easily film,
photograph, and share their discoveries with

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:45.199
the world, so we are creating a microscope
designed to interface with the amazing cameras

00:09:45.199 --> 00:09:47.510
that we all have in our pockets these days.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:52.250
We’re going to be doing this through a Kickstarter
campaign so if you would like to help us make

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:57.620
these microscopes a reality and get one of
your own you’ll need to visit the link in

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:01.850
the description and pledge during the dates
you’re seeing on your screen right now.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:07.370
We’re also creating some materials to help
you in your journey into becoming a microscopist

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:12.529
because, of course, using the microscope is
one part, but also you’ve got collecting

00:10:12.529 --> 00:10:16.600
samples, keeping them healthy, identifying
what you’re seeing, and we want to help

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:17.600
with all of that!

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:22.360
Finding the right microscope can be complicated
and expensive, and we want to help with that

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:26.290
so it’s as easy as possible for you to start
your own journey.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:30.470
If you’re interested in picking one up or
if you just want to know more details about

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:35.550
the microscope itself, you can head on over
to the link in the description.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:39.480
Thank you as always to all of the people on
screen right now.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:42.370
They are our patrons on Patreon.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:46.410
And they are as always the ones who have enabled
us to go on this journey.

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:49.370
So, if you like it, these are the people to
thank.

